24,2,,status,Servas,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
25,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Wow, the yard is a bit of a mess! The 2021 trial failed because people went back to spreadsheets, and trying to switch systems during peak season was a total disaster. We need something really tough!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
26,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings show a worrying trend. Capital requests are being trimmed, while recurring operating costs are approved without question. We must be wary of systems with low entry costs but climbing annual add-on fees.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
27,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"My research reveals a dangerous lack of accountability. During the last outage, support was fragmented across four suppliers, leaving the failure unowned for two days. We cannot afford another ""finger-pointing"" scenario when the system crashes.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
28,2,1,talk,Rin,,"I found some scary things! Our dispatch tool is super old because we skipped upgrades, and that 2018 data move was a mess because outsiders did it and left mistakes behind! We can't let that happen again!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
29,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It is a sobering realization that our institutional memory is fading. We are losing the very people who hold the keys to our systems, and our crews naturally drift back to their own ways when training is too rigid.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
30,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Asuka, if support was that fragmented, we really need a vendor who takes full responsibility! I'm worried that if the new system is too complex, the yard crews will just go back to their spreadsheets again.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Kenji, I agree. Complexity often hides costs. If a system is too difficult to use, we end up paying for ""specialist"" add-ons or consultants just to keep it running, which feeds into the recurring cost problem I found.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Kenji, exactly. A single point of accountability is non-negotiable. Furthermore, @Servas, your point about crews drifting back to old ways is critical; it suggests that any plan not aligned with actual depot routines will fail within months.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Mizuki, @Asuka, that's why we need a system that stays fresh! If we just let it get dusty like the dispatch tool, or let strangers break the data, we'll be right back where we started! It has to be easy to fix!","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""Mizuki"",""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,2,talk,Servas,,"Indeed, Rin. We must remember that a system is only as strong as the hands that guide it. If we ignore the fatigue of our staff, even the most modern tool will become a burden.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Servas, that's so true! If the yard guys hate it, they'll just ignore it. We need a vendor who understands that the freight doesn't stop moving just because we're installing new software!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Rin, you are right about the data. If we rely on external consultants for every update, we are not just risking errors, but creating a permanent, expensive dependency that will bleed our operating budget.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mizuki, that dependency is exactly what leads to the accountability gap I discovered. If the vendor provides a rigid ""out-of-the-box"" solution that doesn't adapt to our depot realities, we will see the same drift we saw in the last rollout.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,3,talk,Rin,,"@Asuka, exactly! It's like when someone cleans my room but puts everything in the wrong place—it's a mess! We need a vendor who actually listens and doesn't just leave us with a broken puzzle to solve.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,3,talk,Servas,,"We must seek a partner who values the wisdom of our veterans and the patience of our learners. A gentle transition, tailored to the rhythm of the depot, will preserve the spirit of our people.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm totally on board with that! Let's find a vendor who is flexible and tough enough for the yard, but simple enough that our team actually wants to use it. I'm ready to see the candidates!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,I agree. We need a solution that is transparent in its pricing and sustainable in its operation. Let us see which of the candidates can offer stability without hidden long-term costs.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,4,talk,Asuka,,I concur. We must prioritize a vendor that offers a clear ownership structure for support and a deployment strategy that respects the actual operational habits of our depots to avoid systemic drift.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,4,talk,Rin,,I'm super excited too! Let's pick someone who won't let our systems get dusty and who actually does the job right the first time! I can't wait to see who they are!,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,4,talk,Servas,,Let us move forward with a spirit of hope. We shall look for the path that honors our workers' time and protects those who carry the heaviest loads. I am ready.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
